  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "What surface is defined by the equation ${z}^{2}=4{r}^{2}$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?",
  "worked_solution": "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}=4{r}^{2}\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that the square of the distance from any point on the surface to the $xy$-plane equals $4$ times the square of the distance from that point to the $z$-axis. In other words, the tangent of the angle between any position vector on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal to\n\n$$\n\\frac{r}{z}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{r}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}}=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{{r}^{2}}{4{r}^{2}}}=\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the tangent of this angle can be a positive or negative number, the $z$-coordinate can also be positive or negative.\n\nOn the other hand, $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in its domain. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the cone shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/assets/7c8d0cefa5eb8da76f6cba59570670e4078c278c801aeb12344dce3183733149.png)",
